text:
Sexing pancreatic cancer samples
Now we can turn our effort towards determining the sex of the pancreatic cancer samples 1-6.
Here we can determine the sex of these samples based on the expression patterns of good old XIST, a transcript that is highly expressed in females and not expressed in males. To be extra sure, we can also include a Y-chromosome gene RPS4Y1 (expected to be expressed in males, not in females). To be extra extra sure, we can include a larger panel of X-chromosome genes (with PCA + clustering).

Subset to samples 1-6 (pancreatic cancer). Generate three graphs, 1. y-axis samples, x-axis XIST expression, barchart. 2. same as graph 1, but x-axis is RPS4Y1 expression. 3. PCA on samples using the top 100 variable XY probes. Each point is a sample.










subsetASampleDomain = subsetAMarkers.map(d =&gt; d.sample).sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b)

function subsetABarChart(gene) {
  return Plot.plot({
    width: 180,
    height: 220,
    style: { background: "transparent", fontSize: "11px" },
    x: { label: `${gene} expression`, grid: true,  tickSize: 0},
    y: { label: "Sample", domain: subsetASampleDomain, padding: 0.2,  tickSize: 0},
    marks: [
      Plot.barX(subsetAMarkers, {
        x: gene,
        y: "sample",
        fill: "#616161",
        title: d =&gt; `Sample ${d.sample}\n${gene}: ${d[gene].toFixed(2)}`
      }),
      Plot.ruleX([0])
    ]
  })
}

subsetAPcaScatter = Plot.plot({
  width: 180,
  height: 220,
  style: { background: "transparent", fontSize: "11px" },
  x: { label: "PC1", tickSize: 0},
  y: { label: "PC2", tickSize: 0},
  marks: [
    Plot.dot(subsetAXyPca, {
      x: "pc1",
      y: "pc2",
      r: 5,
      fill: "#616161",
      title: d =&gt; `Sample ${d.sample}\nPC1: ${d.pc1.toFixed(2)}, PC2: ${d.pc2.toFixed(2)}`
    }),
    Plot.ruleX([0]),
    Plot.ruleY([0])
  ]
})

html`&lt;div style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; gap:1.5rem; align-items:flex-start;"&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:0.25rem;"&gt;XIST expression&lt;/div&gt;${subsetABarChart("XIST")}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:0.25rem;"&gt;RPS4Y1 expression&lt;/div&gt;${subsetABarChart("RPS4Y1")}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:0.25rem;"&gt;PCA, top 100 variable XY probes&lt;/div&gt;${subsetAPcaScatter}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`





























Based on XIST and RPS4Y1 expression, samples 2-3 are likely female, and samples 1, 4, 5, 6 are male. However, the PCA on all XY genes does not separate samples 2/3 from the rest. Cancer is an extremely abnormal process that might be associated with gene expression changes obscuring the expression of sex-specific genes 11.

Generate a plot that shows the a density function of Y chromosome transcript expression - 1 density line for each sample, and color samples 2 and 3 a different color from the rest.







highlightColor = ({ domain: ["Samples 2, 3", "1, 4-6"], range: ["#000000", "#bbbbbb"], legend: true })

subsetAYDensityPlot = Plot.plot({
  width: 500,
  height: 280,
  style: { background: "transparent", fontSize: "11px" },
  x: { label: "Y-chromosome transcript expression" },
  y: { label: "Density", grid: true, tickSize: 0},
  color: highlightColor,
  marks: [
    Plot.line(subsetAYDensity, {
      x: "x",
      y: "density",
      stroke: d =&gt; [2, 3].includes(d.sample) ? "Samples 2, 3" : "1, 4-6",
      z: "sample",
      title: d =&gt; `Sample ${d.sample}`
    }),
    Plot.ruleY([0])
  ]
})

html`&lt;div style="display:flex; justify-content:center;"&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div style="font-weight:600; margin-bottom:0.25rem;"&gt;Y-chromosome transcript density per sample&lt;/div&gt;${subsetAYDensityPlot}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`






















Y-chromosome transcripts are specific to males. The density plot shows that samples 2 and 3 have mostly background-level signal at Y-chromosome transcripts, indicating that these samples are likely female. They appear distinct from samples 1, 4-6, which have a “flatter” distribution, with more Y-chromosome transcripts having above-background levels of expression.
This analysis finalizes our answers. In the table below, I show every sample in this dataset, a column corresponding to each analysis, and final classification (“My Call”). I then added Anthropic’s answer, and another column indicating if I got the answer right for each sample (I didn’t, let’s see why).

Generate a heatmap that shows male/female based on my analysis (platform, tissue, marker analysis), annotate with anthropics answer. Calculate a final accuracy (% correct).










grayShades = ({
  platform: { GPL4133: "#dddddd", GPL887: "#f5f5f5" },
  tissue: { pancreatic: "#dddddd", ovarian: "#f5f5f5" }
})
sexColors = ({ male: "#dbe6f2", female: "#f3dde3", unknown: "#eeeeee" })
matchColors = ({ match: "#000000", "no match": "#ffffff" })

xistScale = d3.scaleSequential(d3.interpolateGreys).domain(d3.extent(subsetAFinalCalls, d =&gt; d.XIST))
rps4y1Scale = d3.scaleSequential(d3.interpolateGreys).domain(d3.extent(subsetAFinalCalls, d =&gt; d.RPS4Y1))

function textColorFor(bg) {
  return d3.lab(bg).l &lt; 55 ? "white" : "black"
}

function finalCallsHeatmap() {
  const columns = [
    { key: "platform", label: "Platform", colors: grayShades.platform, group: "My analysis" },
    { key: "tissue", label: "Tissue", colors: grayShades.tissue, group: "My analysis" },
    { key: "XIST", label: "XIST", scale: xistScale, group: "My analysis" },
    { key: "RPS4Y1", label: "RPS4Y1", scale: rps4y1Scale, group: "My analysis" },
    { key: "my_call", label: "My call", colors: sexColors, group: "My analysis" },
    { key: "anthropic", label: "Anthropic", colors: sexColors, group: null },
    { key: "match", label: "Match", colors: matchColors, group: null }
  ]

  const table = document.createElement("table")
  table.style.cssText = "border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 9px;"

  const groupRow = document.createElement("tr")
  groupRow.appendChild(document.createElement("th"))
  let i = 0
  while (i &lt; columns.length) {
    const group = columns[i].group
    let span = 1
    while (i + span &lt; columns.length && columns[i + span].group === group && group != null) span++
    const th = document.createElement("th")
    th.textContent = group ?? ""
    th.colSpan = group ? span : 1
    // Border only under the actual grouped columns, so the group's real
    // span is visible instead of one line running under the whole table.
    const border = group ? "border-bottom:1px solid #999;" : ""
    th.style.cssText = `padding:1.8px 6px; font-weight:600; text-align:center; ${border}`
    groupRow.appendChild(th)
    i += group ? span : 1
  }
  table.appendChild(groupRow)

  const headerRow = document.createElement("tr")
  headerRow.appendChild(document.createElement("th"))
  for (const col of columns) {
    const th = document.createElement("th")
    th.textContent = col.label
    th.style.cssText = "padding:1.8px 6px; font-weight:600; text-align:center;"
    headerRow.appendChild(th)
  }
  table.appendChild(headerRow)

  for (const d of subsetAFinalCalls) {
    const tr = document.createElement("tr")
    const sampleTd = document.createElement("td")
    sampleTd.textContent = d.sample
    sampleTd.style.cssText = "padding:0.9px 6px; font-weight:600;"
    tr.appendChild(sampleTd)
    for (const col of columns) {
      const value = d[col.key]
      const td = document.createElement("td")
      let bg, text
      if (col.scale) {
        bg = value == null || Number.isNaN(value) ? "#f5f5f5" : col.scale(value)
        text = value == null || Number.isNaN(value) ? "–" : value.toFixed(1)
      } else {
        bg = col.colors[String(value)] ?? "#f5f5f5"
        text = String(value)
      }
      td.textContent = text
      td.title = `Sample ${d.sample} · ${col.label}: ${text}`
      td.style.cssText = `padding:0.9px 6px; text-align:center; background:${bg}; color:${textColorFor(bg)};`
      tr.appendChild(td)
    }
    table.appendChild(tr)
  }
  return table
}

function accuracyCard(d) {
  const box = document.createElement("div")
  box.style.cssText = "border:1px solid black; padding:0.5rem 0.75rem; font-family:monospace; font-size:13.8px; min-width:180px;"
  const label = document.createElement("div")
  label.style.cssText = "font-weight:600; margin-bottom:0.25rem;"
  label.textContent = d.label
  const value = document.createElement("div")
  value.textContent = `Accuracy = ${d.correct}/${d.n} (${d.pct}%)`
  box.append(label, value)
  return box
}

html`&lt;div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center;"&gt;
${finalCallsHeatmap()}
&lt;div style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; gap:0.75rem; margin-top:1rem;"&gt;
  ${subsetAAccuracyCards.map(accuracyCard)}
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`

Render the tab names and content server-side
Next is the server code. The server code does 3 things:

filter the penguins dataset penguin_filtered, based on the radio buttons
dynamically render the ui component based on the island column in the filter penguin dataset i.e. penguin_filtered$island
Create content for each tab, here I chose a histogram over year.


server &lt;- function(session, input, output) {
  # 1. filter by species
  penguins_filtered &lt;- reactive({
    req(input$select_species)
    penguins |&gt; filter(species == input$select_species)
  })

  # 2. create the ui based on the `island` column
  output$dynamic_navset_card &lt;- renderUI({
    nav_items &lt;- unique(penguins_filtered()$island) |&gt;
      purrr::map(
        ~ nav_panel(
          title = .x,
          plotOutput(glue("plot_{.x}"))
        )
      )
    navset_card_pill(!!!nav_items)
  })

  # 3. create the plots
  observe({
    walk(
      unique(penguins_filtered()$island),
      function(x) {
        id &lt;- glue("plot_{x}")
        output[[id]] &lt;- renderPlot({
          penguins_filtered() |&gt;
            filter(island == x) |&gt;
            ggplot(aes(x = year)) +
            geom_histogram(stat = "count") +
            labs(title = glue("Number of penguins by year for island {x}"))
        })
      }
    )
  })
}

The tricky part is figuring out how to dynamically assign the output ids. Here, I programmatically create the ids and then assign them content (plots) based on the relevant subset of data.
